Title: The smoke test confirms the cache exists and reads nothing in it
Created: 2026-09-13
Author: claude
Closed: autoclosed — PR #550

--- log ---
2026-09-13T18:29Z claude created
2026-09-13T18:31Z claude note Filed from the author's question -- "the smoke test should check the hash of the three generated .sdt, does it?" -- which was right about the gap and named an artefact whose extension does not exist. It does exist: `storage/<key>/.zotero-sdt-cache`, one per attachment, magic \x89SDT\r\n\x1a\n. A repo grep for `.sdt` found nothing and I reported the artefact absent; the file is written by Zotero's native code, not by anything in this tree, so the grep was measuring the wrong thing. Found by re-running the smoke test with --keep and looking.

2026-09-13T18:51Z claude note The author read the first green run and said the fixtures were too easy -- three generated 590-byte PDFs, byte-identical to each other, so a pack naming ANY of them satisfied the source-hash check. Drawing the three smallest Menagerie documents instead (137-230 KB, three languages, three distinct MD5s) found a real defect on its FIRST run: two cache records and two packs for three attachments. Not the sitter dropping a document -- the third was still being extracted when the run tore Zotero down. The old fixture always finished inside the census poll, so a one-shot read of a thing still being written measured the clock and called it a pass. The read is now a poll against the artefacts themselves, and a timeout re-raises the last real failure rather than reporting "timed out". Worth recording as the ticket's own lesson: the check was correct and the substrate was too weak to exercise it, which no green run could have revealed.

2026-09-13T19:18Z MinhHaDuong closed — autoclosed — PR #550
--- body ---
## Context

`bench/sitter_smoke_test.py` (ticket 0784) is the only gate that launches a real
Zotero and watches the shipped `.xpi` arm. Its final assertion, at line 301, is:

    cache_path = actual_data_dir / "sdt-sitter-cache.jsonl"
    if not cache_path.exists():

Existence. Nothing reads a row. A cache with zero rows passes; three rows all
recording `empty: true` -- the sitter ran and produced nothing -- passes. This
is the shape `tickets/AGENTS.md` names outright: a check whose all-clear is
indistinguishable from "I could not look" is not a check.

Meanwhile Zotero writes the real product, one per attachment, at
`storage/<key>/.zotero-sdt-cache`. Observed on 2026-09-13 against 10.0.1, three
fixture documents, all three present. Header is 24 bytes -- an 8-byte magic,
then `01 01 01 00` and three little-endian uint32 -- and a raw-deflate stream
begins at offset 40. It decompresses to:

    {"processor":{"type":"pdf","version":3},
     "dateCreated":"2026-09-13T18:27:27.611Z",
     "source":{"contentType":"application/pdf",
               "hash":"c28ebc2294e92415647827bfeb9d567f",
               "properties":{"PDFFormatVersion":"1.4"}}}

## What must NOT be asserted, and why

**Not the file's own digest.** The three fixture PDFs are byte-identical
(`bench/fixtures/smoke_library.py` calls `write_pdf` with no per-item content,
and all three hash to `c28ebc22...`), yet their packs hashed to
`c156e5c9...`, `dcdd4dac...` and `7e2d5b42...` in one run, at 462, 462 and 463
bytes. The whole difference is `dateCreated`, a millisecond timestamp; the size
split is deflate reacting to different digits. A pinned file hash would go red
on its second run and teach every reader to ignore it.

What IS pinnable is the pack's own `source.hash` -- Zotero's record of the bytes
it read, which must equal the MD5 of the fixture this run wrote.

## Relevant files

- `bench/sitter_smoke_test.py` -- line 300-305, the assertion to replace
- `bench/fixtures/smoke_library.py` -- writes the three PDFs whose MD5 is the
  expected `source.hash`; note all three are identical, so this fixture cannot
  catch a per-document mix-up (see Invariants)
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js:344` -- `createSDTCache`, the row schema:
  `{signature, fingerprint, sourceBytes, pages, empty, sample}`

## Actions

1. After the cache-exists check, read `sdt-sitter-cache.jsonl` back: assert one
   row per imported attachment, each with `record.empty === false` and
   `record.pages > 0`, and that every row's `versions` stamp agrees.
2. Enumerate `storage/*/.zotero-sdt-cache`; assert the count equals the
   attachments imported, and that each carries the SDT magic.
3. Decompress each pack's metadata and assert `source.hash` equals the MD5 of
   the fixture PDF this run wrote. Locate the deflate stream by scanning
   offsets rather than hardcoding 40: 10.0.2 changes the SDT version and may
   move the header.
4. Return `processor.version` and the cache's `SDT_SCHEMA_VERSION` /
   `SDT_PACK_VERSION` in the run's JSON result, so a cross-version run can diff
   them instead of re-deriving them by hand.

## Test

Red step first, and it is cheap: assert `source.hash` against a deliberately
wrong MD5 and confirm FAIL; then against the real fixture and confirm PASS.
Separately, delete one `.zotero-sdt-cache` before the assertion and confirm the
count check fails -- that is the arm that proves step 2 is not satisfied by
construction.

## Verification

- [ ] Both red arms above observed failing, then passing
- [ ] `make sitter-smoke` still PASSes against 10.0.1 end to end
- [ ] `make check` green

## Invariants

- The pack file's own digest is never pinned (see above). If a future reader
  wants byte-level stability, the question to ask upstream is whether
  `dateCreated` can be omitted, not whether this test can be made to tolerate it.
- The source-hash check asserts SET MEMBERSHIP: each pack names one of the
  documents this run handed over. With three distinct Menagerie documents that
  is a real constraint, where three identical generated PDFs made it vacuous.
  It still would not catch a PERMUTATION -- pack A naming document B -- which
  needs the pack mapped to its own attachment through Zotero's item keys. Not
  done, and not pretended to be done.
- The smoke test stays non-cycling and seconds-long: no restart, no replace.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/` is only ever READ by this script.

## Exit criteria

The smoke test reads the packs and the cache rows rather than confirming a
filename, both red arms have been shown, and a run reports the SDT version
numbers it observed. This is what lets the 10.0.2 carry-over arm distinguish a
re-preparation from stale rows silently reused -- which it cannot do today.
